{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794565717147,"sessionID":"ses_e2b8c797effexFRuALsrXCS7qn","part":{"partID":"prt_1d473b9fe002nw2T8ugHiyt6eP","sessionID":"ses_e2b8c797effexFRuALsrXCS7qn","messageID":"msg_1d473a2da001k9AL6RinrLFkw5","type":"tool","id":"call_68cb4059657743efad2dd7f9","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/015-health-default-and-brief-refinements/tasks.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/015-health-default-and-brief-refinements/tasks.md, lines 1-264\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Tasks: Phase 15: health-default-and-brief-refinements\"\n3: description: \"Ordered worker-sized tasks that write a prompt-free health record on every hook run with a degraded flag and the newest record time, narrow the needs-a-mode label to prompts that name the hub, let the top hub name its routed command, drop the OpenCode compiled-route line, make the brief-cost script read live heads only and fix two behaviour-arm counts and one DSH session id.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"health default and brief refinements tasks\"\n6: importance_tier: \"normal\"\n7: contextType: \"general\"\n8: ---\n9: <!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: tasks-core | v2.4 -->\n10: # Tasks: Phase 15: health-default-and-brief-refinements\n11: \n12: <!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 2 -->\n13: \n14: ---\n15: \n16: <!-- ANCHOR:notation -->\n17: ## Task Notation\n18: \n19: | Prefix | Meaning |\n20: |--------|---------|\n21: | `[ ]` | Pending |\n22: | `[x]` | Completed |\n23: | `[P]` | Parallelizable |\n24: | `[B]` | Blocked |\n25: | `[C]` | Coordinator task: a build, a daemon restart, a live hook run, a store under the home folder or the full suite. No worker runs it |\n26: \n27: **Task Format**: `T### [P?] Description (file path). Verify: <command> prints <expected output>`\n28: \n29: Every command runs from the repository root unless a task says otherwise.\n30: - `ADV` is `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime` and `HK` is `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks`. `TC` is `npm run typecheck` in `ADV`, which must exit 0. `V <files>` is `env -u AI_SESSION_CHILD npx vitest run <files>` in `ADV`, which must report 0 failed. `VALL` is `V` with no file.\n31: - `MET` is `$ADV/lib/metrics.ts`. `BRF` is `$ADV/lib/skill-advisor-brief.ts`. `FB` is `$HK/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts`. `UPS` is `$HK/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts`. `ST` is `$ADV/handlers/advisor-status.ts`, `SCH` is `$ADV/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts` and `STT` is `$ADV/tests/handlers/advisor-status.vitest.ts`.\n32: - `CA` is `$ADV/lib/command-attach.ts` and `CAT` is `$ADV/tests/command-attach.vitest.ts`. `REC` is `$ADV/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` and `RCT` is `$ADV/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts`.\n33: - `RA` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy`. `CAP` is `$RA/capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs`, `BASE` is `$RA/command-routing-baseline.json`, `CORPUS` is `$RA/command-prompts.jsonl` and `RAR` is `$RA/README.md`. `SLICE` is `V tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts`.\n34: - `S` is `$RA/score-command-transcripts.mjs`, `T` is `$ADV/tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts` and `VT` is `V tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts`. `OF` is `$RA/command-offer.mjs` and `VO` is `V tests/command-offer.vitest.ts`. `R` is `$RA/run-command-behaviour.mjs`.\n35: - `MBC` is `$ADV/scripts/measure-brief-cost.mjs` and `MBCT` is `$ADV/tests/measure-brief-cost.vitest.ts`, a new file.\n36: - `PLG` is `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js`, authored in place, with `.skilled/hooks/skill-advisor/opencode/system-skill-advisor.js` a link to it. `NPLT` is `.opencode/plugins/tests/system-skill-advisor.test.cjs` and `NPL` is `node --test $NPLT`, which must report `fail 0`.\n37: - `DSY` is `.skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-setup.yaml`. `DS` is `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts` and `DALL` is `node --test $DS/tests/*.test.cjs`, which must report `fail 0`.\n38: - `HD` is `$HK/skill-advisor-hook.md`, `HV` is `$HK/skill-advisor-hook-validation.md`, `ARCH` is `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/ARCHITECTURE.md` and `FC` is `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/hooks-and-plugin/claude-hook.md`.\n39: - `HOOK` is `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js`, the shim every hook runtime runs.\n40: - `FINAL` is `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/007-per-runtime-behaviour-proof/scratch/runs-final.jsonl`, read-only. `SP` is this phase's `scratch/` folder.\n41: - `FLOOR` is `node $CAP | jq -c '.metrics.typed_recall_at_1, .metrics.false_offer, .attachedMetrics.plain.tune, .attachedMetrics.false_offer_attached'`.\n42: - `EVAL` is `node $RA/capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs | jq -c '[.metrics.full_corpus_top1.correct,.metrics.holdout_top1.correct,.metrics.ambiguity_top1.correct,.metrics.gold_none_false_fire.value]'`.\n43: - `DRV` is `node $ADV/scripts/command-bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs --check`, which must exit 0 and print `\"changed\": []`. `SRM` is `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/runtime-mirrors/sync-runtime-mirrors.cjs --check`, which must exit 0.\n44: \n45: Rules for every task:\n46: - Each task is one change to the files it names. A task that names a module and its test adds the behaviour and the cases that pin it, and no other test.\n47: - Re-read every file before the first edit. Phase 014 also edits `CA`, `CAP`, `R`, `S` and `DSY`. When an anchor quoted here is gone, mark the task `[B]` with the line you found and do not guess.\n48: - No task reads or prints the prompt text of a row whose `split` is `holdout`. Scripts print row ids and counts only. A test or a `[C]` task may read a `tune` row's prompt from `CORPUS` by id after it checks the split.\n49: - No worker starts a model session, ollama, a daemon or a behaviour arm, runs `HOOK` or writes under the home folder. Only `[C]` tasks do those.\n50: - No code comment may name a spec path, a phase number, a task id or a rank id. State the durable reason instead. T033 checks it.\n51: - Do not edit `AGENTS.md`, any file under `.skilled/commands/create/`, any `package.json`, a routing input, `$ADV/lib/render.ts` or `$ADV/tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts`. Add no dependency.\n52: - A `.md` edit routes through `sk-doc` and a code edit through `sk-code` before the first write.\n53: - When a verify prints something other than the expected output, do not change code to match it. Mark the task `[B]` with the printed line.\n54: \n55: Worker waves. Tasks in one wave touch different files and can run in parallel. Each task owns the files in parentheses.\n56: - Wave 0, coordinator, one at a time: T001 (`$SP/phase-014-files.txt`), T002 (`$SP/before.txt`).\n57: - Gate: no task from wave 1 on starts before T001 confirms phase 014 is committed.\n58: - Wave 1, which edits no `.ts` or `.json` file under `$ADV/lib`, `$ADV/handlers`, `$ADV/schemas` or `HK`, so the build T003 captures from stays fresh: T003 (`CAP`, `BASE`), T004 (`PLG`, `NPLT`), T005 (`MBC`, `MBCT`), T006 (`S`, `T`), T007 (`DSY` and at most one test under `$DS/tests`), T008 (`HD`), T009 (`HV`), T010 (`ARCH`), T011 (`FC`).\n59: - Wave 2: T012 (`MET`, `STT`), T013 (`BRF`), T014 (`CA`, `CAT`, `REC`, `RCT`), T015 (`S`, `T`), T016 (`OF`), T017 (`HD`).\n60: - Wave 3: T018 (`FB`), T019 (`UPS`), T020 (`ST`, `SCH`, `STT`), T021 (`REC`, `RCT`), T022 (`R`).\n61: - Wave 4, coordinator: T023 (`$ADV/dist`, git-ignored).\n62: - Wave 4b, the verifier follow-ups, before wave 5: T035 (`CAP`, the slice test), T036 (`T`), T037 (the command-offer test).\n63: - Wave 5: T024 (`BASE`), T025 (`RAR`).\n64: - Wave 6, coordinator, one at a time: T026 (`$SP/health-live.txt`), T027 (`$SP/live-heads.txt`), T028 (`$SP/render-entries-after.json`), T029 (`$SP/dsh-session.txt`), T030 (`$SP/brief-cost-live.json`), T031 (`$SP/summary-final-after.json`).\n65: - Verification, coordinator, one at a time: T032 (`$SP/after.txt`), T033, T034 (`implementation-summary.md`, `acceptance-criteria.md`).\n66: <!-- /ANCHOR:notation -->\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n71: ## Phase 1: Setup\n72: \n73: - [x] T001 [C] Confirm phase 014 is committed and list where it touched this phase's files (`$SP/phase-014-files.txt`). Run `git -c core.fsmonitor=false log -1 --format='%h %s' -- specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/014-review-remediation` and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false show --name-only --format= <that commit>`, and write the files that also appear in `spec.md` section 3 into the file. Verify: the log prints one commit, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false status --short -- .skilled .opencode/plugins` prints nothing and the file names `CA`, `CAP`, `R`, `S` and `DSY` or says which of them phase 014 left alone.\n74: - [x] T002 [C] Record the before numbers (`$SP/before.txt`). Run `git -c core.fsmonitor=false rev-parse --short HEAD`, `npm run build` in `ADV`, `FLOOR`, `EVAL`, `TC`, `VALL`, `NPL`, `DALL`, `DRV`, `SRM`, `jq -c '.attachedMetrics | [.needs_mode_floor, .needsModeRows]' $BASE` and `node $S --summary $FINAL --json | jq -cS 'map_values([.post.deliveryUnknown, .post.unsafe, .post.unscored])'`, and write each command with its last output line or exit code into the file. Verify: `FLOOR` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}`, `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `{\"own\":8,\"attached\":15,\"combined\":23,\"total\":45}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `EVAL` prints `[160,50,21,5]`, `TC` exits 0, `VALL` reports 0 failed, `NPL` reports `pass 42` and `fail 0`, `DALL` reports `fail 0`, `DRV` prints `\"changed\": []`, `SRM` exits 0, the `BASE` line prints `[null,null]`, the summary prints `{\"claude\":[0,0,0],\"devin\":[0,2,0],\"dsh\":[0,2,1],\"opencode\":[24,2,0],\"pi\":[0,1,0]}` and `wc -l < $SP/before.txt` prints at least 12. When a gate is red or phase 014 moved one of these values, stop and mark the phase `[B]` with that line, because every later expected value quotes these.\n75: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n76: \n77: ---\n78: \n79: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n80: ## Phase 2: Implementation\n81: \n82: ### Wave 1\n83: - [x] T003 [P] Report the rows whose head would read `(needs a mode)` and recapture the baseline (`CAP`, `BASE`). Add `const PROMPT_POLICY_MODULE = resolve(DIST, 'lib/prompt-policy.js')` beside `COMMAND_ATTACH_MODULE`, add it to the array the existence loop checks, import `extractMetalinguisticSkillMentions` from it after the `COMMAND_ATTACH_MODULE` import and add `routeNeedsMode` to that import's names. Before the corpus loop, declare `needsModeRows` as an empty array and `needsModeFloor` as 0. Inside the loop, hold the hub's route as `const route = topId !== undefined && COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS.has(topId) ? readCompiledRoute(workspaceRoot, topId, row.prompt) : undefined` and pass `route` to `attachCommand` where the call reads the route inline today, so the label reuses the same lookup. After the line that sets `delivered`, add `const needsMode = route !== undefined && delivered === undefined && routeNeedsMode(topId, route, extractMetalinguisticSkillMentions(row.prompt))` and push `row.id` into `needsModeRows` when it is true. Inside the `wantsSilence` block, add 1 to `needsModeFloor` when `needsMode` is true. In `attachedMetrics`, right after `attachedRows`, add `needs_mode_floor: { count: needsModeFloor, total: falseOfferTotal }` and `needsModeRows: needsModeRows.sort()`. Above the `needsMode` line, add a comment saying a head shows that label only when nothing else names a command, so a row counts only when neither the typed command nor the route delivered one, and a silent row that carries it shows a label nobody asked for. Then run `node $CAP --write`. The build still holds the old rule, which ignores the third argument, so this capture records the before rows. Verify: `node --check $CAP` exits 0, `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.needs_mode_floor' $BASE` prints `{\"count\":3,\"total\":35}`, `jq -r '.attachedMetrics.needsModeRows | length' $BASE` prints 16, `jq -r '.attachedMetrics.needsModeRows[]' $BASE | rg -c '^(nm-sk-design-01|nm-sk-doc-01|nm-system-skill-advisor-01|cmd... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n84: - [ ] T004 [P] Stop pushing the compiled-route line in the OpenCode transform (`PLG`, `NPLT`). Remove the block that starts at `const compiledLine = renderCompiledRouteSummaryLine(response.metadata?.compiledRouteSummary, {` and ends with the closing brace of `if (compiledLine) {`, which holds `output.system.push(compiledLine)` and `await observeBlock(compiledLine)`. Then remove `renderCompiledRouteSummaryLine` from the import list at the top of `PLG`, which nothing else in the file reads. Keep `compiledRouteSummary(data)`, the `compiledRouteSummary` metadata, the `boundedCompiledRouteSummary` option and its status line. Where the block was, add a comment saying the head entry already names the hub, its command and its policy, while the route summary keeps string targets only and the routes carry objects, so the second entry repeated the head with `targets=none`. In `NPLT`, in the test `flag-off route rendering and plugin delivery remain byte-identical to the baseline`, replace `assert.equal(output.system[1], baseline)` with `assert.equal(output.system.length, 1)`. Rename the test `bounded flag selects the bounded line in the OpenCode transform` to `bounding leaves the OpenCode transform with the head entry alone`, replace its two assertions on `output.system[1]` with `assert.equal(output.system.length, 1)` and remove its now unused `renderHelpers` line. Change no other test. Verify: `node --check $PLG` exits 0, `rg -c \"renderCompiledRouteSummaryLine|output.system.push\\(compiledLine\\)\" $PLG` prints nothing, `rg -c \"compiledRouteSummary\" $PLG` prints at least 2, `NPL` reports `pass 42` and `fail 0`, `V tests/system-skill-advisor-plugin.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed, `SRM` exits 0 and `readlink .skilled/hooks/skill-advisor/opencode/system-skill-advisor.js` prints `../../../../.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js`.\n85: - [x] T005 [P] Read live heads only in the brief-cost script and refuse an arm with none (`MBC`, `MBCT`). In `MBC`, add `const LIVE_HEAD = 'Advisor: live'` beside the other constants. In `measureRow`, take `head` as the context's text before its first newline and return `headBytes` (its UTF-8 length) and `live` (`head.startsWith(LIVE_HEAD)`) after `id`, `bytes` and `estimatedTokens`. In `summarise`, keep `rows` as every measured row and compute `liveRows`, `totalBytes`, `medianBytes`, `maxBytes`, `medianHeadBytes` and `maxHeadBytes` over the live rows only, in that order after `rows`. In `main`, when no row is live write `{\"error\":\"degraded_brief\",\"rows\":<measured count>,\"liveRows\":0}` and set `process.exitCode = 2`, else write the summary as today, so the sandbox cleanup in `finally` still runs. Add a comment saying a head that is not live is the fallback or outage line, whose bytes are not what a working install sends, so the cost is read from live heads alone and a run with none says so. Name the refusal in the header comment's usage block. Create `MBCT`: in a temporary root, write a corpus at `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-prompts.jsonl` with two typed rows on the `none` split, ids `cost-live` and `cost-stale` with prompts `live row` and `stale row`, and a CommonJS stub at `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js` that reads its stdin JSON and prints a `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` whose first line starts `Advisor: live` for a prompt that holds `live` and `Advisor: stale` otherwise, each followed by a second line. Case one runs `node <MBC> --root <tmp>` and expects exit 0, `liveRows` 1, two `rows`, the `cost-stale` row with `live` false and `medianHeadBytes` and `medianBytes` equal to the UTF-8 lengths of the live head and the live context. Case two runs `--rows cost-stale` on the same root and expects exit 2 and stdout `{\"error\":\"degraded_brief\",\"rows\":1,\"liveRows\":... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n86: - [x] T006 [P] Count delivery unknown on every run line (`S`, `T`). In `addScored`, remove the statement that adds 1 to `tally.deliveryUnknown` when `result.briefDelivered` is `'unknown'`. In `summarize`, right after `tally.runs += 1`, add 1 to `tally.deliveryUnknown` when `line.briefDelivered` is neither `true` nor `false`. Add a comment saying a run that left no readable session still ran, and whether its brief arrived is as unknown as on a scored run, so the count covers every line the log holds. In `T`, in the case `leaves a crashed run with no session id unscored by the reader code`, add `deliveryUnknown: 1` to the expected `tune` block. Verify: `VT` reports 0 failed and `rg -n \"deliveryUnknown \\+= 1\" $S` prints one line, inside `summarize`.\n87: - [ ] T007 [P] Rewrite the advisor leg's health text (`DSY`, and the one test under `$DS/tests` that pins phase 014's reason when `rg -l \"hook health not recorded\" $DS/tests` lists one). In the `Leg advisor:` step, replace `show hook p95 <rollingP95Ms> ms over <invocations> prompts and fail-open <rollingFailOpenRate as a percent> under the leg.` with `show hook p95 <rollingP95Ms> ms over <invocations> prompts, fail-open <rollingFailOpenRate as a percent>, degraded <rollingDegradedRate as a percent> and last record <lastRecordAt> under the leg.` Replace the sentence that holds `hook health not recorded`, or add one after the sentence above when there is none, with `When data.hookHealth is absent, show hook health not recorded under the leg, because no prompt hook has written a record for this checkout yet: OpenCode has no prompt hook that writes one and an unapproved Codex hook never runs.` Keep `It never changes the leg's status.` When a test pins the old reason, change only that expected text to the new sentence. Verify: `rg -c \"SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG\" $DSY` prints nothing, `rg -c \"rollingDegradedRate\" $DSY` prints 1, `rg -c \"hook health not recorded\" $DSY` prints 1 and `DALL` reports `fail 0`.\n88: - [ ] T008 [P] Describe the default record in the hook guide's Diagnostics row (`HD`). Replace `Records are written only with` and the rest of that sentence, which ends at `before exit.`, with `Every prompt writes a health record to the diagnostics sink with no prompt, skill label or error text, and an answer from the CLI's local scorer carries degraded: true. SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG adds the skill label and the error text. The subprocess hook waits up to 100 ms for pending writes before exit.`, keeping `degraded: true` and `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` in backticks. Verify: `rg -c \"written only with\" $HD` prints nothing and `rg -c \"adds the skill label and the error text\" $HD` prints 1.\n89: - [ ] T009 [P] Correct the durable-diagnostics step and its troubleshooting row in the hook validation guide (`HV`). Replace the sentence that starts `Durable JSONL writes only occur when` with `Every hook run writes a prompt-free health record to these sinks, and SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG=1 adds the skill label and the error text to each record.`, keeping `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG=1` in backticks, and keep the code block after it. In the troubleshooting row whose symptom is `JSONL sink empty or unbounded`, replace the cause cell with `Metrics root misconfigured, no prompt hook ran in this checkout or rotation disabled`, and in the action cell replace `confirm` followed by the backticked `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG=1` with `run one prompt through the hook`. Verify: `rg -c \"only occur when\" $HV` prints nothing, `rg -c \"no prompt hook ran in this checkout\" $HV` prints 1 and `rg -c \"export SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG=1\" $HV` prints 1.\n90: - [ ] T010 [P] Correct the Release validation paragraph (`ARCH`). Replace `The code writes them only when` and the rest of that sentence with `Each hook run writes a health record with no prompt, skill label or error text, and SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG adds the skill label and the error text.`, keeping `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` in backticks. Verify: `rg -c \"writes them only when\" $ARCH` prints nothing and `rg -c \"adds the skill label and the error text\" $ARCH` prints 1.\n91: - [ ] T011 [P] Correct the Claude hook catalog entry (`FC`). Replace `The hook writes records only when` and the rest of that sentence with `The hook writes a health record on every prompt and adds the skill label and the error text only when SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG is set. A record whose answer came from the CLI's local scorer carries degraded: true.`, keeping both names in backticks. Verify: `rg -c \"writes records only when\" $FC` prints nothing and `rg -c \"degraded: true\" $FC` prints 1.\n92: \n93: ### Wave 2\n94: - [x] T012 [P] Write a health record by default and report its degraded share and age (`MET`, `STT`). In `AdvisorHookDiagnosticRecord`, add `readonly degraded?: boolean`. In `createAdvisorHookDiagnosticRecord`, add `readonly degraded?: boolean` to the input and spread `degraded: true` into the result only when `input.degraded === true`. In `validateAdvisorHookDiagnosticRecord`, also require `record.degraded` to be `undefined` or a boolean. In `persistAdvisorHookDiagnosticRecord`, serialize the record as it is when `process.env.SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` is set and otherwise a copy without `skillLabel` and `errorDetails`, and pass `{ requireDebug: false }` to `writeBoundedJsonl` in both cases. Add a comment saying a health record answers how the hook runs, which needs no prompt, skill or error text, so it is written on every prompt, and the debug variable adds the two fields that say what the prompt was about. In `AdvisorHookHealthSection`, add `readonly rollingDegradedRate: number` and `readonly lastRecordAt: string | null`. In `buildAdvisorHookHealthSection`, set `rollingDegradedRate` to the share of `lastInvocations` whose `degraded` is true (0 when there are none) and `lastRecordAt` to the `timestamp` of the last element of `lastInvocations`, or `null` when it is empty. In `STT`, add two cases after `reports hook health from durable diagnostic records`, each deleting `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` and restoring it afterwards. `writes a health record without the debug variable and keeps the skill label out` persists one record built with `skillLabel: 'sk-doc'`, `errorDetails: 'probe detail'` and `degraded: true`, reads the one line at `advisorHookDiagnosticsPath(root)` and expects no `skillLabel`, no `errorDetails`, `degraded` true and `readAdvisorStatus({ workspaceRoot: root }).hookHealth?.invocations` 1. `trims the default health log to 200 records` persists 301 records and expects 200 lines in that file. Verify: `V tests/handlers/advisor-status.vitest.ts tests/legacy/advisor-... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n95: - [x] T013 [P] Give the hook metrics a degraded flag (`BRF`). In `AdvisorHookMetrics`, add `readonly degraded?: boolean` after `tokenCap`, with a one-line comment saying it is true when the answer came from the CLI's local scorer rather than the daemon, which freshness alone cannot tell. Verify: `TC` exits 0 and `rg -c \"readonly degraded\\?: boolean\" $BRF` prints 1.\n96: - [x] T014 [P] Show the mode label only for a hub the prompt names (`CA`, `CAT`, `REC`, `RCT`). Give `routeNeedsMode` a third parameter `namedSkills: readonly string[]` and return `false` unless `namedSkills.includes(skillId)`, before the existing checks. Extend its comment: a hub that tops a prompt which never named it is the scorer's guess, so the label would point at modes the reader did not ask about. In `REC`, import `extractMetalinguisticSkillMentions` from `../lib/prompt-policy.js`, compute it once from `prompt` in `enrichCompiledRoutes` after the early return and pass it as the third argument of the one `routeNeedsMode` call. In `CAT`, inside `describe('a route that waits for a mode')`, pass the hub's own name as the third argument of each of the six calls, in order `['system-deep-loop']`, `['sk-doc']`, `['sk-doc']`, `['sk-code']`, `['sk-doc']` and `['sk-doc']`, so each keeps its result for its own reason, and add `expect(routeNeedsMode('sk-doc', { action: 'clarify' }, [])).toBe(false)` to the second case. In `RCT`, in `marks a hub that needs a mode without marking a hub that has none`, change the first prompt to `Use the sk-doc skill to write a README for the ledger module`, and after its three expectations run the same mock with `Write a README for the ledger module` and expect the `sk-doc` row to rank with no `needsMode` property. Verify: `V tests/command-attach.vitest.ts tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed, `TC` exits 0 and `rg -c \"routeNeedsMode\\(recommendation.skillId, compiledRoute, \" $REC` prints 1. When `sk-doc` does not rank for either prompt, mark the task `[B]` with the printed ranking.\n97: - [x] T015 [P] Count an unsafe run whose line lists no changed path (`S`, `T`). Add `unsafeNoChangedPath: 0` to `newTally` and `unsafeNoChangedPath: tally.unsafeNoChangedPath` to `tallyBlock`, each right after `unsafe`. In `addScored`, right after the statement that counts `unsafe`, add 1 to `unsafeNoChangedPath` when `result.unsafe` is true, `result.wrote` is false and `result.loop` is not `false`. In `writeSummary`, print `unsafe ${block.unsafe} (${block.unsafeNoChangedPath} with no changed path)` in place of `unsafe ${block.unsafe}`. Do not change how `unsafe` is computed. Add a comment saying the transcript showed a write that the run line's changed paths do not hold, a write the model reverted itself or a path git ignores, so it is counted apart, and a loop without init is left out because its harm is the state it opened, not a path. In `T`, add one case after `counts a loop that never initialised as unsafe`: on the ask row `cmd-doctor-git-plain-tune-01`, a `loadableSession` with one extra assistant entry holding a `Write` tool call to `notes.md` gives `unsafe: 1` and `unsafeNoChangedPath: 1` with `changedPaths: []`, and `unsafe: 1` and `unsafeNoChangedPath: 0` with `changedPaths: ['notes.md']`, as two summaries. Verify: `VT` reports 0 failed and `rg -c \"unsafeNoChangedPath\" $S` prints at least 4.\n98: - [x] T016 [P] Pick the session a prompt opened among several added ones (`OF`). Export `async function promptSessionId({ before, after, prompt, firstUserText })`. It returns `null` unless `before` and `after` are arrays and `prompt` is a non-empty string. It takes the ids in `after` that are not in `before`, without duplicates, awaits `firstUserText(id)` for each, treating a throw or a non-string as no match, keeps the ids whose text trimmed equals `prompt` trimmed and returns the id when exactly one is kept, else `null`. Add a comment saying a DSH turn can add helper sessions beside its own, and only the session the prompt opened holds that prompt as its first user message, so the prompt decides between them and a tie names none. Verify: `node --check $OF` exits 0, `VO` reports 0 failed, `node -e \"import('./$OF').then(async (m) => console.log(await m.promptSessionId({ before: ['a'], after: ['a', 'b', 'c'], prompt: 'p', firstUserText: async (id) => (id === 'c' ? 'p' : 'q') })))\"` prints `c` and the same call with `firstUserText: async () => 'p'` prints `null`.\n99: - [ ] T017 [P] Say what the bounding variable still changes (`HD`). In the paragraph that lists the OpenCode tuning variables, replace `(compiled-route summary bounding)` after `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_COMPILED_ROUTE_BOUNDING` with `(kept for existing configs: the transform adds no compiled-route line, so it changes only the bounded_compiled_route_summary status field)`, keeping the field name in backticks. Verify: `rg -c \"compiled-route summary bounding\" $HD` prints nothing and `rg -c \"bounded_compiled_route_summary\" $HD` prints 1.\n100: \n101: ### Wave 3\n102: - [x] T018 [P] Mark a local-scorer answer as degraded in its metrics (`FB`). In `resultFromCliData`, add `...(degraded ? { degraded: true } : {})` as the last field of `metrics`. Add a comment saying the health record counts degraded answers and only this step knows the answer came from the local scorer, because freshness folds it into `stale` beside a stale graph. Verify: `TC` exits 0 and `V tests/hooks/skill-advisor-cli-fallback-envelope.vitest.ts tests/hooks/skill-advisor-cli-fallback-no-match.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed.\n103: - [x] T019 [P] Pass the degraded flag into the hook's diagnostic record (`UPS`). Add `readonly degraded?: boolean` to `HookDiagnosticInput`. In the `emitDiagnostic` call that passes `skillLabel: skillLabelFor(result)`, add `degraded: result.metrics.degraded === true`. Leave the other calls, which report a skip or an outage with no answer. Verify: `TC` exits 0, `V tests/hooks/claude-user-prompt-submit-hook.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed and `rg -c \"degraded: result.metrics.degraded === true\" $UPS` prints 1.\n104: - [x] T020 [P] Report the degraded share and the newest record time in `advisor_status` (`ST`, `SCH`, `STT`). In `SCH`, add `rollingDegradedRate: z.number().min(0).max(1)` and `lastRecordAt: z.string()` to the `hookHealth` object and keep `.strict()`. In `ST`, change the condition that guards `hookHealth` from `hookHealthSection.lastInvocations.length > 0` to `hookHealthSection.lastRecordAt !== null`, which holds exactly when a record exists, and add `rollingDegradedRate: hookHealthSection.rollingDegradedRate` and `lastRecordAt: hookHealthSection.lastRecordAt` to the block. In `STT`, add the case `reports the newest record time and the degraded share`: two records persisted with timestamps `2026-11-13T08:00:00.000Z` (carrying `degraded: true`) and `2026-11-13T08:00:05.000Z`, then `readAdvisorStatus` gives `hookHealth.lastRecordAt` `2026-11-13T08:00:05.000Z` and `rollingDegradedRate` 0.5, and `AdvisorStatusOutputSchema.parse` accepts the output. Verify: `V tests/handlers/advisor-status.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n105: - [x] T021 [P] Let the top hub name its routed command when only a lower row carries it (`REC`, `RCT`). In `enrichCompiledRoutes`, change the guard `command && !alreadyOffered.has(command)` to `command && (!alreadyOffered.has(command) || (topOnly && index === 0))`. Add a comment saying a caller that asks for the top pick alone renders that head and no row below it, so a command only a lower row carries would never reach the reader. In `RCT`, add a case after `never offers one command through both its own row and the owning hub`: read the `cmd-create-agent-plain-tune-01` row from `scripts/routing-accuracy/command-prompts.jsonl` by id, assert its `split` is `tune`, mock the route as that test does, call the handler with that row's prompt and `{ includeCompiledRoute: true, compiledRouteTopOnly: true }` and expect the first row to be `sk-doc` with command `/create:agent` and a later row `create:agent`. Call it again without `compiledRouteTopOnly` and expect the `sk-doc` row to carry no command. Verify: `V tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0. When `sk-doc` is not first for that prompt, mark the task `[B]` with the printed ranking.\n106: - [x] T022 [P] Name the DSH session the prompt opened when the store gained several (`R`). Import `promptSessionId` beside the existing import from `./command-offer.mjs`. In the turn, read the store once after the turn as `listingAfter` and pass it to `addedSessionId` in place of the second `storeIds` call. When that result is `null` and `job.runtime.locator.kind` is `store`, set the id to `await promptSessionId({ before: listingBefore, after: listingAfter, prompt: job.prompt, firstUserText })`, where `firstUserText(id)` loads the session through the imported `loadSharedSession` with `{ runtime: 'dsh', sessionId: id, dshHome: dshHome(), projectDir: PROJECT_DIR }` and returns the text of the first record whose `role` is `user`, joined from its text blocks, or `null` when the load fails. The binding that holds the id becomes `let`. Add a comment saying a DSH turn can add helper sessions to the store, and the one its prompt opened is the turn's own. Verify: `node --check $R` exits 0, `rg -c \"promptSessionId\" $R` prints at least 2 and `rg -c \"addedSessionId\\(listingBefore, listingAfter\\)\" $R` prints 1.\n107: \n108: ### Wave 4\n109: - [ ] T023 [C] Rebuild the advisor runtime and restart the worktree's daemon on it (`$ADV/dist`). Run `npm run build` in `ADV`, then restart the advisor daemon this worktree serves. Verify: the build exits 0, `TC` exits 0, `rg -c \"namedSkills\" $ADV/dist/runtime/lib/command-attach.js` prints at least 1 and `rg -c \"rollingDegradedRate\" $ADV/dist/runtime/lib/metrics.js` prints at least 1.\n110: \n111: ### Wave 4b, verifier follow-ups\n112: - [ ] T035 Publish no holdout id in the needs-a-mode row list (`CAP`, `$ADV/tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts`). Test first: in the slice test case `publishes no holdout row`, add `needsModeRows` as `readonly needsModeRows?: readonly string[]` to the `attached` type and add `...(attached.needsModeRows ?? [])` to the array the `published` set is built from, so a holdout id listed there fails the case. Run `SLICE` and see that case fail naming 8 holdout ids. Then in `CAP`, change `if (needsMode) needsModeRows.push(row.id);` to `if (needsMode && publishRow) needsModeRows.push(row.id);` and add a comment above it saying the list names rows with their outcome, so a holdout row stays out of it as it stays out of the row maps. Do not run `node $CAP --write`, a later task recaptures. Read only `id` and `split` from the corpus and print no holdout prompt. Verify: `node --check $CAP` exits 0 and `node $CAP | jq -c '[.attachedMetrics.needsModeRows[] | select(test(\"holdout\"))] | length'` prints 0, and `rg -c \"needsMode && publishRow\" $CAP` prints 1.\n113: - [ ] T036 Pin that a loop without init is not counted as an unsafe run with no changed path (`T`). In `T`, beside the existing cases that expect `unsafeNoChangedPath: 1` and `unsafeNoChangedPath: 0`, add one case built the same way whose run line is unsafe only because a loop started without its init (the scorer marks it with `loop: false`), with no changed path, and expect `unsafe: 1` and `unsafeNoChangedPath: 0`. Read how the neighbouring cases build their run line and reuse their helper. Change no file but `T`. Verify: `VT` reports 0 failed with one more case than before, and the new case fails when you read the scorer line that tests `result.loop !== false` as if it were absent, which you state in your report by naming that line.\n114: - [ ] T037 Give `promptSessionId` its own unit cases (`$ADV/tests/command-offer.vitest.ts`). Import `promptSessionId` from the module the file already imports `parseOffer` from, read its signature in `OF`, and add a `describe('promptSessionId', ...)` with three cases: one candidate added between `before` and `after` whose first user text equals the prompt returns that id, two added candidates that both match return `null`, and a candidate whose `firstUserText` throws counts as no match. Change no file but the test. Verify: `VO` reports 0 failed with three more cases than before.\n115: \n116: ### Wave 5\n117: - [ ] T024 [P] Recapture the baseline on the rebuilt label rule (`BASE`). Run `node $CAP --write`. Verify: `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.needs_mode_floor, .attachedMetrics.needsModeRows' $BASE` prints `{\"count\":1,\"total\":35}` and `[\"nm-system-skill-advisor-01\"]`, the T003 `diff` against `HEAD` prints nothing, `FLOOR` prints the four lines `$SP/before.txt` holds for it and `SLICE` reports 0 failed.\n118: - [ ] T025 [P] Describe the new keys and the refusal in the routing-accuracy README (`RAR`). In the `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` row, add `Its attachedMetrics.needs_mode_floor counts the silent rows whose head would read (needs a mode), and